    {
      "id": "7c4efd5e-67cc-46ff-a940-0cd3eb178d07",
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Can you explain to me how C# classes work?",
      "createdAt": "2026-09-14T01:36:15.468Z"
    },
    {
      "id": "3401cab2-ad63-461e-b63b-26a491cf929b",
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "**How C# classes work**\n\n- **Definition**  \n  A C# class is a *reference type* that describes a design for a set of objects.  \n  It declares fields, properties, methods, constructors, events, and other members that together define the state and behavior a created object will have.  \n  Every class implicitly derives from `System.Object`, which supplies common functionality such as `ToString()`, `GetHashCode()`, and `Equals()` [1].\n\n- **Creating an instance**  \n  When the `new` operator is used, a constructor is run.  \n  The constructor has the same name as the class, no return type, and can accept parameters to initialize the new object’s state [4].  \n  Multiple constructors can be overloaded, and one constructor may delegate to another to centralize common initialization logic.\n\n- **State vs. behavior**  \n  - *Instance fields / properties* hold the object’s own data; each instance gets its own copy.  \n  - *Methods* are shared by all instances; they operate on the instance data via the `this` reference.\n\n- **Inheritance**  \n  A class can inherit from another class (or implement interfaces).  \n  The derived class gains the base class’s members, can override virtual members, and can add its own members.\n\n- **Visibility and access**  \n  Members can be declared `public`, `private`, `protected`, `internal`, or combinations thereof, controlling how they can be accessed from outside the class or assembly.\n\n- **Class libraries**  \n  A class can be packaged into a class library (DLL). Other projects reference the library’s assembly, import its namespaces, and then create objects of its public classes [5].\n\nIn short, a C# class is a blueprint that defines the structure (state) and capabilities (behavior) of objects; constructors set up the initial state, and all objects share the same method definitions.",
      "thinking": "Need explain classes using source 1, constructor 4, inheritance from Object, etc.",
